== a_fill_by_a_number
error[Z0401]: `area` fills each area in one colour, so `fill` must show categories, but `lo` is a Float without value labels, which is shown on a continuous scale
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:31
  |
2 | d.plot(x: t, y: v).area(fill: lo)
  |                               ^^ a colour for each row, not for each area
  |
  = help: fill by a variable of categories, as in `area(fill: union)`, or set one fill for this layer, as in `area(fill: "steelblue")`

== a_fill_by_a_number_from_the_plot
error[Z0401]: `ribbon` fills each band in one colour, so `fill` must show categories, but `lo` is a Float without value labels, which is shown on a continuous scale
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:30
  |
2 | d.plot(x: t, y: v, fill: lo).ribbon(low: lo, high: hi)
  |                              ^^^^^^ a colour for each row, not for each band
  |
  = help: fill by a variable of categories, as in `ribbon(fill: union)`, or set one fill for this layer, as in `ribbon(fill: "steelblue")`

== not_a_colour
error[Z0401]: `fill:` of `area` is "blu", which is not the name of a colour
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:31
  |
2 | d.plot(x: t, y: v).area(fill: "blu")
  |                               ^^^^^ not a colour
  |
  = help: did you mean "blue"?

== a_variable_in_quotes
error[Z0401]: `fill:` of `ribbon` is "g", which is not the name of a colour
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:52
  |
2 | d.plot(x: t, y: v).ribbon(low: lo, high: hi, fill: "g")
  |                                                    ^^^ not a colour
  |
  = help: a string sets one colour for every mark; to colour by the variable `g`, write it without quotation marks, `fill: g`

== an_edge_that_is_text
error[Z0401]: `ribbon` draws `high` on a linear scale, so `g` must be a Bool, Int or Float variable, but it is a Str
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:42
  |
2 | d.plot(x: t, y: v).ribbon(low: lo, high: g)
  |                                          ^ not a numeric variable
  |
  = help: the edges of a band are numbers; `d.describe()` lists the types of the variables

== no_such_edge
error[Z0101]: no variable `hii` in `d`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:42
  |
2 | d.plot(x: t, y: v).ribbon(low: lo, high: hii)
  |                                          ^^^ not a variable of d
  |
  = help: did you mean `hi`? If you meant a category level, write it in quotes: `"hi"`

== another_kind_of_fill
note: 1 row with NA in t or v dropped (line 2)
note: 1 break in an area where v is NA (line 2)
error[Z0401]: the plot shows `g` as categories for `fill`, but `t`, which `ribbon` maps to `fill`, is an Int without value labels, which is shown on a continuous scale
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:68
  |
2 | d.plot(x: t, y: v, fill: g).area().ribbon(low: lo, high: hi, fill: t)
  |                                                                    ^ `t` is of another kind
  |
  = help: the layers of a plot share one scale of fill, so the variables mapped to `fill` must all be categories (a Category, a Str, a Bool or a variable with value labels) or all be numbers without value labels

== alpha_above_one
error[Z0401]: `alpha:` of `area` is an opacity between 0 and 1, but this is 1.5
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:32
  |
2 | d.plot(x: t, y: v).area(alpha: 1.5)
  |                                ^^^ outside [0, 1]
  |
  = help: write 1 for a fill that hides what is under it, or a share such as 0.6 so that it shows through

== alpha_below_zero
error[Z0401]: `alpha:` of `ribbon` is an opacity between 0 and 1, but this is -0.1
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:53
  |
2 | d.plot(x: t, y: v).ribbon(low: lo, high: hi, alpha: -0.1)
  |                                                     ^^^^ outside [0, 1]
  |
  = help: write 1 for a fill that hides what is under it, or a share such as 0.3 so that it shows through

== no_color_on_area
error[Z0404]: `area` has no argument called `color`
 --> scripts/main.zeff:2:25
  |
2 | d.plot(x: t, y: v).area(color: g)
  |                         ^^^^^ unknown argument
  |
  = help: usage: `p.area(data: Frame, x: Var | Expr, y: Var | Expr, fill: Var | Str, group: Var, alpha: Float = 0.6, position: overlay | stack | fill = overlay)`

